      "question": "I want to know if Transformer 1 at MAIN is running hot compared with its load. Over the available data window, summarize top-oil temperature (oti), its alarm and trip readings (oti_a, oti_t), and the three phase currents (il1, il2, il3) with neutral current (inut), giving min, max and mean for each. Then tell me whether the highest oti reading happened at the same timestamp as the highest total phase current. Flag any phase whose mean current differs from the other phases' average by more than 10%. If oti has no data, use ati for context and say that the thermal correlation can't be confirmed.",
      "answer": "**Transformer 1 (MAIN) — summary over 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19 (5 daily records):**\n\n| Sensor | Min | Max | Mean |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| oti (°C) | 84 | 87 | 85.4 |\n| oti_a | 1 | 2 | 1.8 |\n| oti_t | 1 | 1 | 1.0 |\n| il1 (A) | 100 | 102 | 100.6 |\n| il2 (A) | 103 | 106 | 104.6 |\n| il3 (A) | 94 | 96 | 95.0 |\n| inut (A) | 5 | 6 | 5.2 |\n\n**Thermal vs load correlation:**\n- Highest oti = 87 °C at 2024-01-16.\n- Total phase current (il1+il2+il3): Jan-15 = 300, Jan-16 = 301, Jan-17 = 299, Jan-18 = 301, Jan-19 = 300.\n- The highest oti (Jan-16) coincides with the highest total phase current (301 A), which is tied with Jan-18 (301 A, oti 86 °C). So yes — the peak oil temperature occurred at a peak-load timestamp, consistent with load-driven heating, though the current variation is small (~0.7% range).\n\n**Phase imbalance check (>10% deviation from the other phases' average):**\n- il1: others' avg = (104.6+95.0)/2 = 99.8 → deviation +0.8%\n- il2: others' avg = (100.6+95.0)/2 = 97.8 → deviation +7.0%\n- il3: others' avg = (100.6+104.6)/2 = 102.6 → deviation −7.4%\n\nNo phase exceeds the 10% threshold — no significant phase imbalance flagged.\n\n**Concerns:** Despite modest loading (mean currents ~100 A on a 100 MVA-rated unit, load nearly flat), oti is high (84–87 °C) and both the alarm flag (oti_a, mean 1.8 — mostly active) and trip flag (oti_t = 1 on every reading) are set. The transformer is running hot relative to its load; the thermal correlation with load can be confirmed (peak oti at peak current), but the elevated temperature at low load variation suggests an issue beyond load alone (e.g., cooling system, ambient — note ati/ambient averages 78 °C, which is unusually high). Further investigation of cooling and ambient conditions is recommended.",
